-- title:
--   Elements of the dilatation A[I/a] are the fractions g/aⁿ, g∈ Iⁿ
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative ring, let $I \subseteq A$ be an ideal, let $a \in A$ be an element of $I$, and let $x$ be an element of the localisation `Localization.Away a` of $A$ at the powers of $a$. The assertion is an equivalence. On one side stands membership of $x$ in [`AffineDilatation.subalgebra I a`](def/RingTheory_AffineDilatation.html#L17), by definition the $A$-subalgebra `Algebra.adjoin A (gen I a)` of `Localization.Away a` generated by the set `gen I a`, whose elements are the fractions $g/a$ with $g \in I$. On the other side stands the existence of a natural number $n$ and an element $g \in A$ with $g \in I^n$ such that the localisation fraction `IsLocalization.mk'` of numerator $g$ and denominator the element $a^n$ of the submonoid `Submonoid.powers a` equals $x$; that is, $x = g/a^n$ with $g \in I^n$. No restriction is placed on $a$ beyond $a \in I$ (in particular $a$ may be a zero divisor or zero), and $n = 0$ is allowed, so that the elements of the image of $A$ are included.
--
--   This is the standard identification of the affine dilatation (affine blowup) algebra $A[I/a]$, defined as the subalgebra of $A[1/a]$ generated by the fractions $g/a$ for $g \in I$, with the degree-zero part of the Rees algebra $\bigoplus_n I^n$ inverted at $a$, namely the set of fractions $g/a^n$ with $g \in I^n$. It is the computational description used by [`AffineDilatation.exists_algHom_isLocalization_map`](thm.html#AffineDilatation.exists_algHom_isLocalization_map) to produce maps out of the dilatation, and more generally to compute dilatations after localisation or base change.
-- source:
